Description: Expose workspaces to X11 clients as EWMH desktops, like i3
 X11 pagers, taskbars and tools such as wmctrl and xdotool find
 workspaces through the EWMH desktop properties on the root window, and
 switch desktops or move windows with EWMH client messages. In the i3
 session they work; under sway's Xwayland they saw no desktops at all:
 wmctrl -d printed nothing and xdotool set_desktop refused to run.
 .
 sway now publishes _NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS, _NET_DESKTOP_NAMES,
 _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP and _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT on the Xwayland root
 window and _NET_WM_DESKTOP on each managed X11 window, and adds them to
 _NET_SUPPORTED. Desktops are the workspaces, ordered by output position
 (left to right, then top to bottom) and by IPC order within an output.
 Sticky floating windows and hidden scratchpad windows report
 0xFFFFFFFF. _NET_WM_DESKTOP is deleted when a window is unmapped.
 .
 The _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP and _NET_WM_DESKTOP client messages switch
 desktops and move windows without changing the focused workspace;
 0xFFFFFFFF makes a tiled window floating and sticky. A _NET_WM_DESKTOP
 set before mapping chooses where the window appears; assign criteria
 take precedence, as in i3 4.25.
 .
 Squashed from the four commits sent upstream; they apply to 1.11
 unchanged. The first one splits "move container" in move.c so that
 windows are moved by workspace pointer rather than by name.
